1
00:00:02,595 --> 00:00:04,670
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:04,671 --> 00:00:08,430
In this chapter, let's learn about light and color.

3
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:13,310
This time, let's talk about the theoretical content of light and color.

4
00:00:16,944 --> 00:00:19,770
Light and color are inseparable.

5
00:00:19,771 --> 00:00:22,650
Because there's light, there's also color.

6
00:00:22,651 --> 00:00:29,171
Even the same object takes on red under red light, and blue under blue light.

7
00:00:29,345 --> 00:00:31,690
It also varies depending on the material.

8
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:37,210
Light and color should always be in a relationship that influences each other.

9
00:00:37,211 --> 00:00:44,250
For example, let's take a figure bathed in sunset light, cut out the person, and paste them into a clear photo.

10
00:00:44,945 --> 00:00:47,130
They'd be floating there, all yellow and red.

11
00:00:48,655 --> 00:00:54,801
After adjusting the color to fit the environment, it at least looks more in place.

12
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:57,640
This is how lighting environment affects color.

13
00:01:01,210 --> 00:01:05,180
In a drawing, light must follow a certain rule.

14
00:01:05,181 --> 00:01:07,820
When we draw, we set up the light.

15
00:01:08,514 --> 00:01:12,380
If you've set up the light, you have to maintain that setting.

16
00:01:12,381 --> 00:01:18,140
For instance, if light comes from the right on the face, it can't come from the left on the body.

17
00:01:19,444 --> 00:01:30,750
I've seen many cases where people ignore this and draw from memory, so the face ends up with a familiar light applied, while the body doesn't match, creating a sense of disconnect between them.

18
00:01:30,751 --> 00:01:34,750
Unless it's a special situation, always keep it unified.

19
00:01:34,751 --> 00:01:40,871
You might think, what's the point of saying something so obvious? Surprisingly, a lot of people don't follow this well.

20
00:01:41,534 --> 00:01:44,730
I drew a simple example here.

21
00:01:45,639 --> 00:01:47,930
It's a drawing where light falls on a nose.

22
00:01:47,931 --> 00:01:50,410
Many people like this lighting setup.

23
00:01:50,625 --> 00:01:54,170
The light is coming down almost vertically.

24
00:01:54,171 --> 00:01:55,930
Did you sense the problem?

25
00:01:55,931 --> 00:02:01,050
The problem with this picture is that the light wasn't used consistently.

26
00:02:01,051 --> 00:02:07,370
It's the result of combining a familiar face coloring and a familiar body coloring without a standard for light.

27
00:02:07,371 --> 00:02:13,710
Even if it looks okay for now, my drawing skill will stay stuck here, and I won't be able to move forward.

28
00:02:13,711 --> 00:02:17,470
There's a big difference between drawing with knowledge and drawing without it.

29
00:02:17,471 --> 00:02:21,550
First, you need to understand why the light hits the nose this way.

30
00:02:21,551 --> 00:02:30,670
It's a case where the light falls nearly vertically, and while the light is blocked by the hair, only the nose, which sticks out the most on the face, receives the light.

31
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:35,470
This kind of distinct shadow boundary can't appear on the neck.

32
00:02:35,471 --> 00:02:40,670
For a shadow like this to form, the light would have to come in at an angle like this.

33
00:02:42,170 --> 00:02:45,941
In that case, there's no way the nose would get that stripped-down, empty feeling.

34
00:02:47,310 --> 00:02:49,470
The body below it is the same too.

35
00:02:49,471 --> 00:02:52,030
It's the same light setup as the shadow I drew on the neck.

36
00:02:52,915 --> 00:02:56,471
So the body is using a different light setup than the face.

37
00:02:56,945 --> 00:03:01,090
I've redrawn this picture to match the light setup.

38
00:03:02,359 --> 00:03:07,770
Even if this picture doesn't look hugely wrong, you can feel a certain disconnect somewhere.

39
00:03:07,771 --> 00:03:11,251
Especially as the rendering progresses, it will get more pronounced.

40
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:17,450
Rather than this kind of habitual coloring, sometimes you need to be a bit calculated.

41
00:03:17,451 --> 00:03:20,611
So you can draw under a variety of lighting conditions.

42
00:03:23,799 --> 00:03:27,130
Objects have their own color and value.

43
00:03:27,131 --> 00:03:28,650
Let's call it inherent value.

44
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:31,290
You have to keep this inherent value well.

45
00:03:31,291 --> 00:03:34,211
You've probably drawn a cylinder at school or an academy.

46
00:03:35,135 --> 00:03:41,450
They teach you that if darkness and brightness go from 0 to 10, you should cram in everything from 0 to 10.

47
00:03:41,451 --> 00:03:44,170
That's not a good way to learn shading.

48
00:03:44,171 --> 00:03:50,010
They're just trying to get you accustomed to extreme exercises of dark and light with a pencil, and to gradients.

49
00:03:50,011 --> 00:03:54,090
If we apply that to our drawings, we get very bad pictures.

50
00:03:54,091 --> 00:03:57,290
It becomes a picture with strong contrast even though it's not metal.

51
00:03:57,291 --> 00:04:03,530
Keep the inherent value well: dark materials go dark, medium materials go medium.

52
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:07,871
Bright materials stay relatively bright. That's the rule.

53
00:04:08,874 --> 00:04:10,230
It's extremely important.

54
00:04:10,231 --> 00:04:13,791
You should always color while thinking about this inherent value.

55
00:04:14,970 --> 00:04:20,779
It means that skin should be brighter than jeans, and a t-shirt should always look brighter than skin.

56
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:27,550
Light can also strengthen or weaken color.

57
00:04:27,551 --> 00:04:32,510
For instance, if a warm-colored object receives warm-colored light, that color is reinforced.

58
00:04:32,511 --> 00:04:37,550
Conversely, if a blue object receives warm light, the blue will look dulled.

59
00:04:37,551 --> 00:04:40,951
You can easily observe this under a sunset sky.

60
00:04:41,395 --> 00:04:51,070
The side receiving the red sunset light will glow yellow and red, and the opposite side will receive the blue sky light, creating bluish shadow and shade.

61
00:04:51,071 --> 00:04:52,350
The same goes for a clear daytime.

62
00:04:52,430 --> 00:05:04,110
A surface receiving bright, pure white light will have its color come alive vividly, while a dark surface and shadows that don't will pick up the reflected blue sky color and other surrounding colors.

63
00:05:04,111 --> 00:05:07,790
Let's recall why shadows are blue outdoors.

64
00:05:07,791 --> 00:05:11,630
In a snowfield, you can observe this phenomenon more clearly.

65
00:05:11,631 --> 00:05:16,270
Under a clear blue sky, the shadows on a snowfield are blue, influenced by the sky.

66
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:21,790
Under an overcast sky, the shadows take on a muted color due to the influence of the cloud light.

67
00:05:22,465 --> 00:05:24,700
Let's check with a drawing.

68
00:05:24,701 --> 00:05:26,780
This is a drawing assuming a clear sky.

69
00:05:27,025 --> 00:05:33,100
So, a drawing using a bright, slightly warm sun light and a blue fill light.

70
00:05:33,101 --> 00:05:36,940
This is a drawing assuming an overcast sky.

71
00:05:36,941 --> 00:05:45,020
Together with the object's own desaturated colors, the reflected light is also desaturated, so a low-saturation picture was completed overall.

72
00:05:45,021 --> 00:05:49,100
I tend to mainly use the qualities of sunlight in my drawings.

73
00:05:49,101 --> 00:05:51,500
Because it's the light most familiar to people.

74
00:05:52,235 --> 00:05:55,551
Let's always remember the comfort that comes from familiarity.

75
00:05:56,045 --> 00:05:58,710
On a clear day, the boundaries of shadows are distinct.

76
00:05:59,270 --> 00:06:02,550
Because sunlight travels in a straight line.

77
00:06:02,551 --> 00:06:08,790
Dark areas touching the sky receive the blue sky light and take on a blue tint.

78
00:06:08,791 --> 00:06:13,110
Bright areas are so strongly lit by the sun that the blue reflected light can't exert itself.

79
00:06:14,845 --> 00:06:22,040
To explain overcast days compared to clear days: first, the sun, the light source, gets scattered as it passes through the clouds.

80
00:06:22,041 --> 00:06:27,640
Because of the light scattered by the clouds, the shadow boundaries become blurry and soft.

81
00:06:28,390 --> 00:06:31,560
Because the light splits up and reaches everything evenly.

82
00:06:31,561 --> 00:06:38,040
Since the blue sky light can't pass through the clouds, no blue reflected light forms either.

83
00:06:38,041 --> 00:06:45,960
There are clear pros and cons: it has less impact than a strong lighting setup, but it's easier on the eyes, and there's its own melancholic atmosphere.

84
00:06:46,630 --> 00:06:55,080
I tend not to use scattered light like on overcast days, because my taste favors clear and intense light.

85
00:06:55,081 --> 00:07:01,960
When I work on concept art, I usually set up a strong light coming from one side and a reflected light on the opposite side.

86
00:07:02,335 --> 00:07:05,320
The advantage of this is that the light becomes rich.

87
00:07:05,321 --> 00:07:11,080
The more places light reaches, the more I can bring out the volume of each element.

88
00:07:11,081 --> 00:07:12,680
I sometimes use backlight too.

89
00:07:12,770 --> 00:07:18,720
For concept art that doubles as mood painting, I use it when I want to give the character a sense of pressure.

90
00:07:18,721 --> 00:07:27,520
When using backlight in concept art, the strong light comes from behind the character, but even then I place a soft light in front so it doesn't get too dark.

91
00:07:30,865 --> 00:07:36,090
Light can be expressed in various ways depending on which material it reflects off of.

92
00:07:36,091 --> 00:07:46,010
Basically, light from a light source at an angle of incidence reaches our eyes along an angle of reflection based on the normal.

93
00:07:46,011 --> 00:07:50,531
The normal is the line drawn perpendicular to the object.

94
00:07:50,845 --> 00:07:54,810
If the angle of incidence changes, the angle of reflection changes too.

95
00:07:55,455 --> 00:07:58,010
The rougher the material, the more diffuse reflection occurs.

96
00:07:58,895 --> 00:08:01,530
Let me explain with examples.

97
00:08:01,531 --> 00:08:05,251
The line drawn perpendicular to the surface is called the normal, right?

98
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:14,250
Based on this normal, the light bends, and if the surface is this rough, the light scatters.

99
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:20,810
You can see this in various materials, and you can see it in skin, leather, and rough metal too.

100
00:08:20,811 --> 00:08:25,530
Skin looks smooth on the outside, but under a microscope the surface is bumpy.

101
00:08:26,655 --> 00:08:30,730
In most materials around us, diffuse reflection occurs.

102
00:08:30,731 --> 00:08:34,531
The light doesn't concentrate but scatters, so the contrast is relatively low.

103
00:08:35,539 --> 00:08:41,460
Conversely, if the surface is smooth, the place the light reflects is concentrated, so the contrast looks strong.

104
00:08:41,461 --> 00:08:45,101
Smooth metal or plastic, for example.

105
00:08:45,380 --> 00:08:50,141
If the surface is smooth, it's easy to pinpoint the highlight.

106
00:08:52,170 --> 00:08:55,571
If the surface is rough, it's hard to pinpoint the highlight.

107
00:08:57,365 --> 00:09:00,750
The same goes for curved surfaces.

108
00:09:00,751 --> 00:09:07,390
Depending on the surface material, that is, the roughness of the surface, the rendering has to differ.

109
00:09:07,391 --> 00:09:13,630
It's like how celebrities have a glow on their face, but someone like me with dull skin doesn't.

110
00:09:13,631 --> 00:09:16,590
Another important thing is total internal reflection.

111
00:09:16,591 --> 00:09:19,150
It's a physics term, and it's hard.

112
00:09:19,230 --> 00:09:23,351
The gist is that as the angle of incidence gets bigger, the reflectance gets higher.

113
00:09:24,515 --> 00:09:31,470
As you can see, the bigger the angle of incidence, the higher the reflectance.

114
00:09:31,471 --> 00:09:34,910
Beyond a certain angle, it totally reflects.

115
00:09:34,911 --> 00:09:37,550
Let's do an experiment in a place with a fluorescent light.

116
00:09:39,995 --> 00:09:42,680
There's a cute duck behind a transparent plastic sheet.

117
00:09:42,681 --> 00:09:45,601
I took this photo under a fluorescent light.

118
00:09:45,645 --> 00:09:50,040
The fluorescent light passes through the plastic and you can see the duck.

119
00:09:50,041 --> 00:09:54,680
But what if you move the position to increase the angle of reflection?

120
00:09:54,681 --> 00:10:02,520
The reflectance of the plastic rises, so the fluorescent light can't pass through and is totally reflected.

121
00:10:02,521 --> 00:10:03,720
The duck has disappeared.

122
00:10:04,445 --> 00:10:16,120
The reason you can see pebbles on the bottom through the clear water near the bank, but the farther you look, the more the trees, mountains, and sky around you are reflected, is also because of total internal reflection.

123
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:22,360
This can be used very effectively in drawings, so it's good to draw with it in mind.

124
00:10:22,361 --> 00:10:26,360
I brought a photo where total internal reflection is clearly visible.

125
00:10:26,361 --> 00:10:38,839
As you can see, on the near side, light passes through the water, reaches the pebbles, and reflects back, so we can see the pebbles.

126
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:44,350
But the farther you look, the bigger the angle of incidence of the light gets.

127
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:53,450
Light doesn't pass through the water but reflects, and the water surface becomes a mirror.

128
00:10:53,451 --> 00:10:56,410
Light varies depending on the situation.

129
00:10:56,411 --> 00:11:02,531
Let's study the light we can observe around us in our own way and apply it to our drawings.

130
00:11:05,590 --> 00:11:09,160
I told you color is inseparable from light.

131
00:11:09,161 --> 00:11:10,520
Light has color too.

132
00:11:11,325 --> 00:11:14,641
In illustration, you usually unify it into one color family.

133
00:11:15,320 --> 00:11:18,840
Depending on the color of the light, you cover the whole thing with the same color family.

134
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:24,840
It makes the picture feel stable and lets you focus on the elements in the picture.

135
00:11:24,841 --> 00:11:31,160
You can also deliberately make a color pop. If there's a part that pops, the gaze naturally goes there.

136
00:11:31,161 --> 00:11:33,400
Use it when you want to focus attention.

137
00:11:34,139 --> 00:11:38,620
In concept art, you should refrain from giving color to the light.

138
00:11:38,621 --> 00:11:41,500
Because 3D modelers hate it.

139
00:11:41,501 --> 00:11:44,860
Light and color exist in the background too.

140
00:11:44,861 --> 00:11:51,020
Usually you use a lot of white for the background, but sometimes you cover it with black or gray.

141
00:11:51,021 --> 00:11:56,060
Here, the brightness of the character must also change according to this background color.

142
00:11:56,061 --> 00:12:01,260
On a white background, bright, scattered reflected light generally washes over everything, so it gets brighter.

143
00:12:02,550 --> 00:12:09,441
On a dark background, there's relatively less scattered reflected light, so it doesn't wash over.

144
00:12:10,280 --> 00:12:15,000
I moved a picture drawn on a white background to a dark background.

145
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:17,560
It feels a bit like it's floating.

146
00:12:17,561 --> 00:12:24,890
Conversely, I moved a picture drawn on a black background to a white background.

147
00:12:24,890 --> 00:12:27,210
It's become too dark.

148
00:12:27,211 --> 00:12:33,811
Even a meaningless plain background affects the character, so draw keeping that in mind.

149
00:12:36,965 --> 00:12:41,360
When I design, I set the colors broadly, about two tones.

150
00:12:41,361 --> 00:12:45,520
I set a main color and a supporting color.

151
00:12:45,521 --> 00:12:48,880
Set it broadly first, then break it down finely.

152
00:12:48,881 --> 00:12:54,640
I prefer about a 7 to 3 ratio: the main color is 7 and the supporting color is 3.

153
00:12:55,360 --> 00:12:57,600
There are lots of good references around.

154
00:12:57,920 --> 00:13:06,400
For example, a white t-shirt with jeans, gold and red, black and gold, and red and black, and so on.

155
00:13:06,930 --> 00:13:10,100
There are familiar and effective two-tone color schemes.

156
00:13:10,101 --> 00:13:16,420
I use value contrast in these two-tone colors, but I don't use complementary contrast much.

157
00:13:16,421 --> 00:13:21,380
Because I think complementary contrast is a color contrast you don't often see in reality.

158
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:26,740
You can draw good pictures well enough without using many colors.

159
00:13:26,741 --> 00:13:31,700
Because of that, I use complements sparingly, as a point color.

160
00:13:31,701 --> 00:13:34,260
I don't use blue much either.

161
00:13:34,261 --> 00:13:37,480
I've mainly designed medieval-style games.

162
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:39,720
They say blue pigment was expensive back then.

163
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:44,520
So people back then wore a lot of warm-colored clothing.

164
00:13:44,521 --> 00:13:46,520
The East is the same too.

165
00:13:46,521 --> 00:13:51,640
Even today, aside from jeans, I almost never wear blue clothes.

166
00:13:51,641 --> 00:13:55,480
I recommend using colors that are familiar to our eyes.

167
00:13:55,481 --> 00:13:57,080
So it feels real.

168
00:13:57,081 --> 00:14:01,160
But if you use this in reverse, it's good for designing something that doesn't feel real.

169
00:14:01,830 --> 00:14:05,060
That's one of the reasons you see a lot of blue in the sci-fi genre.

170
00:14:05,109 --> 00:14:06,109
Ah.

171
00:14:09,045 --> 00:14:13,310
When drawing skin, I draw with a lot of awareness of the surrounding environment.

172
00:14:13,311 --> 00:14:20,350
If the background is dark, it means there's little light scattering around, so I draw the shadows dark.

173
00:14:20,351 --> 00:14:24,270
If the background is bright, it's because the light is scattering.

174
00:14:24,271 --> 00:14:29,710
I also mix a bright color of the same family into the shadows of the skin.

175
00:14:29,790 --> 00:14:36,590
An object's color is influenced by light, so you shouldn't memorize color codes and color with them.

176
00:14:36,591 --> 00:14:39,591
You always have to think organically and draw.

177
00:14:42,170 --> 00:14:47,260
If the light is strong, the boundary between the lit and shadow areas can come alive in red.

178
00:14:47,340 --> 00:14:49,100
That's called subsurface scattering.

179
00:14:49,980 --> 00:14:51,740
Our skin is semi-translucent.

180
00:14:52,195 --> 00:14:55,740
It's said our reflectance is only about thirty percent.

181
00:14:55,741 --> 00:14:57,740
Most of the light gets absorbed.

182
00:14:59,100 --> 00:15:04,430
If the light is strong, the light absorbed in the skin reflects from within.

183
00:15:06,035 --> 00:15:10,620
Then it comes back out, and at that point it carries the color of the blood.

184
00:15:13,430 --> 00:15:15,480
This creates this phenomenon.

185
00:15:16,760 --> 00:15:20,680
It also serves to soften the edge of the skin.

186
00:15:20,681 --> 00:15:25,960
Thin parts like ears or fingers can also look translucent.

187
00:15:25,961 --> 00:15:28,920
Let's talk more about reflected light.

188
00:15:28,921 --> 00:15:32,840
I said that light of the same family reinforces color.

189
00:15:32,841 --> 00:15:37,000
Observe the color of a finger in the surroundings.

190
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:41,640
The reflected light from the face washes onto the finger, raising its saturation.

191
00:15:42,705 --> 00:15:49,349
This phenomenon isn't limited to skin; it can appear on anything, like clothes or accessories.

192
00:15:50,030 --> 00:15:53,580
The brighter the object, the more it's influenced by its surroundings.

193
00:15:53,581 --> 00:15:55,660
Let's look at a photo.

194
00:15:55,661 --> 00:16:00,700
Here you can see the blue reflected light washing over from the sky.

195
00:16:02,030 --> 00:16:05,150
The arm is the same too.

196
00:16:05,151 --> 00:16:12,110
Even though it's the same darkness, you can see this part is relatively less blue than this part.

197
00:16:12,111 --> 00:16:13,390
I explained total internal reflection before.

198
00:16:14,110 --> 00:16:19,950
If you draw the cross-section of the arm, it's like this, and the blue reflected light comes in this way.

199
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:24,110
Because the angle of incidence is large.

200
00:16:24,111 --> 00:16:26,110
Clothes are the same too.

201
00:16:26,111 --> 00:16:30,190
This part is bluer than this part.

202
00:16:30,191 --> 00:16:36,190
And the red light reflected from the flower pattern on the clothes is washing over even the blue.

203
00:16:38,460 --> 00:16:42,910
Inside the neck, no blue reflected light washes in.

204
00:16:42,911 --> 00:16:44,190
Because it's blocked by the neck.

205
00:16:45,310 --> 00:16:46,750
The inside of the legs is the same too.

206
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:55,210
The light scattered by the clothes makes it a bit brighter than the arm.

207
00:16:55,211 --> 00:17:02,010
If you observe these phenomena well and apply them to your drawing, you can draw more realistically.

208
00:17:02,011 --> 00:17:06,810
Light and color should be approached theoretically and resolved emotionally.

209
00:17:06,811 --> 00:17:17,130
Based on theoretical study, practice realistic light and color, and on top of that, add your own taste as you find even more beautiful light and color.

210
00:17:17,999 --> 00:17:21,970
I think the eye is important in digital drawing.

211
00:17:21,970 --> 00:17:28,369
In hand-drawn work you can't make various attempts in one picture, but digital drawing makes that possible.

212
00:17:28,370 --> 00:17:29,490
That's a blessing.

213
00:17:29,491 --> 00:17:37,570
As long as you have an eye, through many coloring attempts and software adjustments, you'll be able to find the optimal light and color.

214
00:17:37,571 --> 00:17:39,890
Take in a lot of your surroundings in daily life.

215
00:17:39,891 --> 00:17:44,290
And I hope you'll store them in the reference warehouse in your head.

216
00:17:44,291 --> 00:17:47,010
Then the light and color of my drawings will become rich too.

217
00:17:47,875 --> 00:17:52,270
This time, we looked at the theoretical content of light and color.

218
00:17:52,271 --> 00:17:58,390
Next time, we'll look at how to easily apply this light and color to a picture.
